<body>
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

  <p>A polygon is a bidimensional geometrical figure which describes a closed
  area. In MiraMon, it also is a an entity which is topologically described by
  a succession of arcs that define its boundaries. Polygons can have
  alphanumerical attributes associated to them (e.g. the vegetation type that
  is present within the polygon). A possible means of transferring or
  associating these attributes to the polygons is by inserting a point in the
  interior of each polygon. Each point will have all the attributes that we
  want to transfer to the polygon associated to it. These points are named
  labels and the process by which attributes are transferred to the polygons
  is named labelling (<a href="atripol.htm">ATRIPOL</a>, <a href="atritop.htm">ATRITOP</a>). Sometimes though, the inverse process is needed,
  that is, to get the labels needed to label the polygons from the polygons
  themselves.</p>

  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/><br/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.--> 

  <p>Labels can only be generated in a totally authomatic way from a file
  which has topology (POL) or from a file with polygons defined explicitly
  (VEC) which does not have overlapping polygons. In addition, in the case of
  VEC files no polygons can have inner islands (polygons within polygons),
  reason by which, in the case of VEC files, there is an option which checks
  for errors when labels are generated.</p>

  <p>The position of the point in the Y dimension is close to the arithmetic
  mean of its coordinates. The position of the point in the X dimension can be
  established either by a distance which is less or equal to the tolerance
  (indicated in the command line) of the right or left boundary of the polygon
  or as much to the centre as allowed by the geometry of the polygon.</p>

  <p>In the case of a VEC file of explicit polygons, a better result is
  obtained if the tolerance is small and the points get very close tot the
  boundary of the polygons. In contrast, in the case of POL files, we can
  choose to label polygons with the centre option, since this is aesthetically
  better.</p>

  <p>When the original file is a VEC file of explicit polygons the resulting
  files is a VEC file of points. In the case of a POL file, the resulting
  files is of type PNT.</p>
  <hr/>

  <exclusiva>
